Answer: done. The route maker now costs a field by what one ship mines there in one hour, sold once down a station's bids: each price level up to its depth and no further (commit 5dca25f, game/fleet/routemaker.py). A load's figure is the hour's divided by the loads, so a bid of six units counts six units an hour, never six a load. On the books of 18:00 UTC the per-load figure of 22 mine candidates changed (one gas route from 907 cr a load to 455); no estimate per ship-hour and no station changed, because both already counted a bid's depth once. A candidate whose bid is too small says so: `<item>: bid <price> x <units> at <base> takes <n> of <m> units an hour`. The `min_price` of a route file it writes is four fifths of the lowest level the hour reaches, rounded up, never 1 where a bid stands. The fifteen ice routes that sell deuterium ice carry a `min_price` of 156 to 180 cr today (read 18:16 UTC).
Not built: a separate station per item of one load (deuterium ice to one station, water ice to another): a carry route from storage does that today. Switched off: counting what a bid regains per hour on top of its depth. The measure (`hive.py rates`) averages every reading on record, so the large water ice order that left Central Nexus this morning still counted and moved 17 ice fields to a bid of two units; it stays off until the measure reads the last hours only.

